+# gem5 DADAO Arch Notes
+
+**Date**: 2026-07-09
+**gem5 version**: tag `v25.1.0.1`, commit `c8222cc67a399bfc01e8658dd14b30d5bfd634f9`
+**Base**: upstream https://github.com/gem5/gem5.git (fork, pinned)
+**Status**: buildable skeleton — `gem5.opt` builds, a DADAO SE workload starts and
+exits cleanly. No DADAO instruction semantics are implemented yet (every
+instruction decodes to a placeholder Noop that exits the sim loop).
+
+## Build
+
+```bash
+cd ~/DADAO-gem5
+scons build/DADAO/gem5.opt -j$(nproc)     # ~2h cold; incremental relink is fast
+```
+
+`build_opts/DADAO` selects the target:
+
+```
+TARGET_ISA = 'dadao'
+USE_DADAO_ISA=y
+BUILD_ISA=y
+```
+
+## Run (SE smoke test — clean start + exit)
+
+```bash
+python3 tests/dadao/gen_min_elf.py tests/dadao/min.elf     # emit a DADAO ELF
+./build/DADAO/gem5.opt tests/dadao/dadao_se.py tests/dadao/min.elf
+```
+
+Expected tail:
+
+```
+Beginning DADAO SE simulation!
+Exiting @ tick 77000 because DADAO fault
+```
+
+Flow: SEWorkload.init_compatible() recognises the ELF as arch `dadao`
+(e_machine 0xda0) → the DADAO Process::Loader builds a DADAOISA::Process →
+the CPU fetches the entry instruction → decoder returns a Noop placeholder →
+`DADAONoopInst::execute` returns `DADAOSyscallFault` → `DADAOFault::invoke`
+calls `exitSimLoop`. Because decode ignores the raw bytes, the ELF segment
+contents are irrelevant — any well-formed DADAO ELF exits on its first fetch.
+
+## Arch directory layout — `src/arch/dadao/`
+
+| File | Role |
+|------|------|
+| `Kconfig`, `SConscript` | build wiring (`USE_DADAO_ISA`, Source/SimObject lists) |
+| `types.hh` | `DADAOInst { uint32_t inst }` |
+| `pcstate.hh` | `PCState = GenericISA::UPCState<4>` (4-byte insts) |
+| `page_size.hh` | `PageShift=12`, `PageBytes=4KiB` |
+| `registers.hh` | int/float/misc `RegClass`es + ABI RegIds (placeholders) |
+| `utility.hh` | small helpers (placeholder) |
+| `faults.{hh,cc}` | `DADAOFault` (invoke→`exitSimLoop`), `DADAOSyscallFault` |
+| `decoder.{hh,cc}` | `Decoder : InstDecoder`; every inst → `DADAONoopInst` |
+| `isa.{hh,cc}` | `ISA : BaseISA`; pushes the 8 canonical reg classes |
+| `interrupts.{hh,cc}` | `Interrupts : BaseInterrupts`, no interrupts |
+| `mmu.hh`, `tlb.{hh,cc}` | SE-only MMU/TLB, translation via process page table |
+| `process.{hh,cc}` | `Process : gem5::Process` (ProcessParams), minimal argsInit |
+| `se_workload.{hh,cc}` | `SEWorkload : gem5::SEWorkload` + `Process::Loader` registration |
+| `system.{hh,cc}` | `System : gem5::System` (SE uses generic System; this is a stub) |
+| `DADAO*.py` | SimObjects: CPU, Decoder, ISA, Interrupts, MMU, TLB, SeWorkload, System |
+
+### Non-arch touch points (every gem5 arch does this)
+- `build_opts/DADAO`, `src/arch/Kconfig`, `src/arch/SConscript` — target wiring.
+- `src/base/loader/object_file.hh` — `Dadao` added to `enum Arch`.
+- `src/base/loader/object_file.cc` — `archToString(Dadao) == "dadao"`.
+- `src/base/loader/elf_object.cc` — `e_machine 0xda0 && ELFCLASS64 → Dadao`.
+
+## v25.1 API shapes the skeleton must match (reference: `src/arch/riscv`, `src/arch/power`)
+
+- **ISA params namespace** — forward-declare `struct DADAOISAParams;` in
+  `namespace gem5` (NOT inside `DADAOISA`), and `#include "params/DADAOISA.hh"`
+  in isa.cc. A forward decl in the wrong namespace makes a distinct incomplete
+  type that is not derived from `SimObjectParams`.
+- **BaseISA pure virtuals** — must implement `newPCState`, `inUserMode`,
+  `copyRegsFrom` (in addition to the misc-reg accessors), and push the 8 reg
+  classes into `_regClasses` in canonical `RegClassType` order.
+- **Process vs SEWorkload are separate objects** — `SEWorkload` is a SimObject
+  (`gem5::SEWorkload` subclass, gives arch/byteOrder/syscall). `Process` is a
+  separate SimObject built from `ProcessParams` by a global `Process::Loader`
+  singleton (registered in se_workload.cc) keyed on `obj->getArch()`. Do not
+  give Process `SEWorkloadParams`.
+- **`Process::syscall`** is `void syscall(ThreadContext *tc)` in v25.
+- **`MemState`** ctor is 6-arg: `(owner, brk, stackBase, maxStackSize,
+  nextThreadStackBase, mmapEnd)`.
+- **`System(const Params&)`** needs `Params` derived from `SystemParams`, so the
+  Python `DADAOSystem` must subclass `System`, not bare `SimObject`.
+- **Decoder** — `arch/generic/decoder.hh :: InstDecoder` has two pure virtuals:
+  `moreBytes(const PCStateBase&, Addr)` and `decode(PCStateBase&)`; both must be
+  overridden or the SimObject is not constructible. `decoder.hh` also needs the
+  complete `StaticInst` type (`#include "cpu/static_inst.hh"`), not a forward
+  decl, because `decode` returns `StaticInstPtr` by value.
+- **Concrete SEWorkload must override `byteOrder()`** (pure virtual in Workload);
+  DADAO returns `ByteOrder::big`.
+- **CPU needs an MMU** — set `ArchMMU`/`mmu` on the Python CPU classes; the MMU
+  overrides `translateFunctional(start,size,...)` returning an
+  `MMUTranslationGen`, and its TLB delegates SE translation to
+  `tc->getProcessPtr()->pTable->translate(req)`.
+
+## How to add a real instruction (for G1 / DG-002a)
+
+1. **Decode** — in `decoder.cc`, replace the blanket `DADAONoopInst` return in
+   `Decoder::decode(DADAOInst)` with opcode dispatch on `inst.inst` bitfields.
+2. **Execute** — add a `StaticInst` subclass per instruction with:
+   - ctor `Inst(...) : StaticInst("name", enums::<OpClass>)` and populated
+     `_numSrcRegs`/`_numDestRegs`/`_srcRegIdx`/`_destRegIdx`;
+   - `Fault execute(ExecContext *xc, trace::InstRecord*) const override` using
+     `xc->getRegOperand/setRegOperand`, `xc->readMem/writeMem`, etc.;
+   - `generateDisassembly(...)` and `advancePC(...)` (`pc.as<PCState>().advance()`
+     for straight-line, or set npc for branches).
+3. **Register/decode file** — real arches use a `.isa` grammar compiled by the
+   ISA parser; the skeleton hand-writes StaticInsts in decoder.cc, which is the
+   simplest path until the ISA description is ported.
+4. **Registers / ABI** — flesh out `registers.hh` (real reg count, RegNames, ABI
+   RegIds), and give `SEWorkload` a real syscall ABI (`SyscallABI64` +
+   `guest_abi::Result` specialisation) so `Process::syscall` can dispatch to
+   `SyscallDescTable`.
+
+## Loading a flat binary @ 0x80000000 (research note for G1)
+
+The skeleton loads an **ELF** (SE path). To load a raw flat binary at a fixed
+address instead, three options, cheapest first:
+
+1. **Wrap the blob in a one-segment ELF** (what `tests/dadao/gen_min_elf.py`
+   does): a single `PT_LOAD` at `p_vaddr = 0x80000000`, `e_entry` in it,
+   `e_machine = 0xda0`, `ELFCLASS64`, big-endian. Then the existing SE
+   Process/Loader path just works — no gem5 changes. Recommended for bring-up.
+2. **FS-style memory image** — in a config, create the `System`, `memory.write()`
+   the blob at `0x80000000`, set the thread PC to `0x80000000`, and skip the SE
+   Process. Needs an FS-ish config and a DADAO `System`/workload that maps the
+   region; more plumbing than option 1.
+3. **Custom `Workload`/`SEWorkload::loadObject` for flat binaries** — teach
+   `base/loader` a "raw" object type or add a DADAO workload that memmaps the
+   blob and sets entry/PC. Most work; only worth it if the toolchain emits raw
+   images rather than ELFs.
+
+Since the DADAO LLVM toolchain already emits ELF objects (e_machine 0xda0), and
+`lld` can produce linked ELF executables, option 1 (or just using a real linked
+ELF) is the natural route once there is a linker script; a linked executable
+with real code replaces `min.elf` unchanged.
+
+## Verified (2026-07-09)
+
+- `scons build/DADAO/gem5.opt -j6` → `build/DADAO/gem5.opt` (699 MiB), exit 0.
+- `gem5.opt tests/dadao/dadao_se.py tests/dadao/min.elf` →
+  `Exiting @ tick 77000 because DADAO fault`, exit 0.
